"""A spelling-based approximation, dominated by short bet/pet syllables.

An et beginning a token after b/p is especially strong. Nearby syllables
are weaker, and am/em have the opposite sign. Spelling length, capitals,
and whether the syllable crosses a token boundary change its strength.
Only three preceding characters and a weak one-token aftereffect are kept;
this does not attempt to explain the component's small background signals.
